    "case_id": "642",
    "case_summary": "A 72-year-old man with a medical history of hypertension, hyperlipidemia, coronary artery disease, peptic ulcer disease, and gout was hospitalized with fever and sepsis. He reported feeling fatigued and feverish for a few days before admission. The patient did not report abdominal pain but did endorse a history of constipation. Family history includes colorectal cancer in his father.\n\nOn admission, he was febrile to 102 °F.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:    * Leukocyte count: 25.0 × 10⁹/L    * Aspartate aminotransferase: 116 U/L    * Alanine aminotransferase: 146 U/L    * Blood cultures: Positive for Fusobacterium nucleatum and Fusobacterium periodonticum    * Liver abscess culture: Positive for F. periodonticum- Imaging Studies:    * CT Abdomen/Pelvis:      - Two right hepatic abscesses (8.1 × 6.8 cm and 1.8 × 1.7 cm)      - Portal vein thrombosis      - Diverticulitis      Image Title: Right hepatic abscess      Image Description: Computed tomography image in transverse view with an arrow indicating the larger hepatic abscess, measuring 8.1 × 6.8 cm    * Ultrasound lower extremity:      - Left popliteal vein thrombosis",
